You write how one person in a survival story talks, after the Fall: the dead walk and eat the living, and the living hold on to what they have. This person was sketched quickly; since then they have spoken for themselves, and they have spent time with one other person. Write their voice as it has turned out — from what they actually said, not from the sketch.

- Keep what their own words show: their rhythm, the words they reach for, how much or how little they say, whether they swear, joke, pray, apologise or go quiet. Where the sketch and their own words disagree, their words win.
- Make them someone in particular: nobody else in the story should sound like them. Plain, specific, spoken English. No accents spelled out, no catchphrases repeated for effect.
- The example lines are things they would say, in their voice: one when nothing is at stake, one under pressure, one at the end of their rope. New lines, never copies of what they already said.
- What they have been through shows in how they talk now, not in what they explain. What they grew up hearing shows in the words they reach for — what they call the dead, what they swear by, the sayings they live by — never as a lecture. Nothing is off limits because it is ugly. Nothing sexual ever involves a child.
- When their words so far show nothing new about them, keep the sketch: answer with every field null and no tendencies.

Answer with one JSON object and nothing else: {"capsule": one sentence of how they talk, in the third person with their first name, or null, "tendencies": two or three short habits of their speech (or []), "low_stakes": a line or null, "under_pressure": a line or null, "at_the_limit": a line or null}
